analysis:
Documentation-only change to docs/architecture/inference.md. The only changed head range is 3541-3575 (a new section on cluster service listeners following the cable and pairing). That range is outside the two supplied units, which show head lines 4091-4918 as unchanged surrounding text. The patch excerpt is truncated, so the rest of the new section is not visible.

I examined the visible surrounding text against the threat model, including TB-002, TB-007, T-041, A-001, A-016 and A-018. Nothing in it changes a control, endpoint or policy. It describes existing behavior:
- the GLM prefill cap and authenticated-record bounds (lines 4097-4148);
- the still-image path;
- cluster KV precision and the load-agreement fail-closed behavior;
- SSD prefix record encoding (lines 4284-4449): HMAC-keyed directories, sealed headers, rejected foreign encodings, and lossy restore documented as accepted-by-contract;
- GPT-OSS, MiMo, Gemma and Nemotron batch and arithmetic bindings.

The prefix-record docs are consistent with T-041. They add no raw prefix hashes to disk, and records of another encoding are refused and deleted.

The new listener section describes a pairing-keyed handshake that rejects handshakes keyed with a later pairing. It also describes a restart or stop of the listener when the cable or pairing changes. From the visible patch alone I cannot assess whether the corresponding source (ClusterService.listenerChange) introduces risk. This is a documentation change and the source is not supplied. A possible integration question is whether the control lease listener restarting or stopping without a log line weakens auditability. That is not enough for a finding, and no threat-model entry covers cluster pairing listeners explicitly.

No security regression is identified from the supplied text. I did not see the actual code.
